1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"library--vocabulary-size","kind":"library-article","route":"/roadmap/library/vocabulary-size","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/library/vocabulary-size","sourceHash":"2f1f8ef99dfcd24279edb46d3afa56a0a8596c62fbb772b414e37670101a314c","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T23:11:24.709Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"Vocabulary Size","zh":"词汇量"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"segment-0001","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Vocabulary size — how many words you know — is the single strongest predictor of reading comprehension in a second language. In the Refold method, word count milestones are used as rough checkpoints, not as strict requirements.","zh":"词汇量——也就是你认识多少词——是第二语言阅读理解能力最有力的单一预测因素。在 Refold 方法中，词汇量里程碑只是粗略的检查点，并非严格要求。"}},{"id":"the-numbers","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"The Numbers","zh":"具体数字"}},{"id":"segment-0003","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"<strong>1,000 words:</strong> ~70% of most text. You can understand the structure of sentences and many common words, but miss a lot of meaning. (End of Phase 1)","zh":"<strong>1,000 个词：</strong>覆盖大多数文本的约70%。你能理解句子的结构和许多常见词，但会错过大量含义。（第1阶段结束）"},{"en":"<strong>2,000 words:</strong> ~80% of most text. You understand the backbone of sentences. Still uncomfortable. (End of Phase 2A)","zh":"<strong>2,000 个词：</strong>覆盖大多数文本的约80%。你能理解句子的骨架，但仍会感到不自在。（第2A阶段结束）"},{"en":"<strong>3,000 words:</strong> ~85% of texts and content. Starting to follow stories with some support. (End of Phase 2B)","zh":"<strong>3,000 个词：</strong>覆盖文本和内容的约85%。在一定帮助下，你开始能够跟上故事。（第2B阶段结束）"},{"en":"<strong>4,000 words:</strong> ~90% of texts and content. Comfortable reading with subtitles in familiar domains. (End of Phase 2C)","zh":"<strong>4,000 个词：</strong>覆盖文本和内容的约90%。在熟悉的领域里，配合字幕阅读会比较轻松。（第2C阶段结束）"},{"en":"<strong>5,000 words:</strong> ~98% of every day conversation. Comfortable talking about normal topics with natives (Phase 5).","zh":"<strong>5,000 个词：</strong>覆盖日常对话的约98%。可以自在地与母语者谈论普通话题（第5阶段）。"},{"en":"<strong>8,000 words:</strong> ~98% novels and most things you'd want to engage with. Comfortable across most everyday topics. (Phase 6)","zh":"<strong>8,000 个词：</strong>覆盖约98%的小说和大多数你想接触的内容。面对大多数日常话题都能轻松应对。（第6阶段）"},{"en":"<strong>10,000+ words:</strong> ~98%+ of basically everything. Near-native comprehension, but probably still more to learn. (Phase 7)","zh":"<strong>10,000 个词以上：</strong>基本覆盖所有内容的98%以上。理解能力接近母语者，但可能仍有很多需要学习的东西。（第7阶段）"}]},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"These numbers are approximate and vary by language.","zh":"这些数字只是近似值，并且会因语言而异。"}},{"id":"why-it-matters","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Why It Matters","zh":"为什么重要"}},{"id":"segment-0006","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Vocabulary is the foundation everything else is built on. Grammar knowledge doesn't help if you don't know the words. Listening practice doesn't help if you can't recognize the vocabulary. In the early and middle phases, learning more words is almost always the highest-impact thing you can do.","zh":"词汇是其他一切能力的基础。如果不认识词，语法知识也帮不上忙。如果无法识别词汇，听力练习也难以发挥作用。在早期和中期阶段，学习更多词汇几乎总是你能做的影响最大的一件事。"}},{"id":"how-to-count","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"How to Count","zh":"如何计算"}},{"id":"segment-0008","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Most learners don't need an exact count. If you're using Anki, your number of \"mature\" cards gives a rough estimate. Some languages have vocabulary tests online. Or some tools have rough counting methods.","zh":"大多数学习者不需要精确计算。如果你在使用 Anki， “成熟”卡片的数量可以提供一个粗略估计。有些语言网上有词汇量测试，也有一些工具提供粗略的计算方法。"}},{"id":"segment-0009","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"But nothing will be perfect. What is a word, anyway? A rose by any other name would smell as sweet. 🌹","zh":"但没有任何方法会绝对准确。到底什么算一个词？玫瑰即使换个名字，闻起来依然芬芳。🌹"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The exact number matters less than whether you're meeting the comprehension benchmarks described in each sub-phase's \"When to Move On\" section.","zh":"相比精确数字，更重要的是你是否达到了各个子阶段“何时进入下一阶段”部分所描述的理解能力标准。"}},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与依据"}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Vocabulary size is one of the most robust predictors of language comprehension. <a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/learning-vocabulary-in-another-language/491314AA1B451AD04F3536000F1C9F0D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nation (2001)</a>, in his comprehensive review of vocabulary research, established that approximately 1,000 words covers about 70% of everyday written and spoken English, and this pattern holds across many languages. <a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/234651421_Unknown_Vocabulary_Density_and_Reading_Comprehension\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hu and Nation (2000)</a> further demonstrated that learners need roughly 98% lexical coverage for comfortable unassisted reading — a finding that directly supports the vocabulary milestones in the roadmap.","zh":"词汇量是语言理解能力最稳健的预测因素之一。<a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/learning-vocabulary-in-another-language/491314AA1B451AD04F3536000F1C9F0D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nation（2001）</a>在对词汇研究进行综合回顾时指出，大约1,000个词可以覆盖日常英语书面语和口语的约70%，而这一模式在许多语言中都成立。<a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/234651421_Unknown_Vocabulary_Density_and_Reading_Comprehension\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hu 和 Nation（2000）</a>进一步证明，学习者需要大约98%的词汇覆盖率，才能在没有辅助的情况下舒适地阅读——这一发现直接支持路线图中的词汇量里程碑。"}},{"id":"segment-0013","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The research distinction between reading comprehension and listening comprehension matters: <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-349-12396-4_12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Laufer (1992)</a> showed that vocabulary size is the single strongest predictor of reading comprehension, while other factors (prosodic cues, contextual guessing) play larger roles in listening. This explains why the roadmap prioritizes vocabulary in early phases and emphasizes reading-based immersion.","zh":"研究对阅读理解和听力理解的区分很重要：<a href=\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-349-12396-4_12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Laufer（1992）</a>发现，词汇量是阅读理解能力最有力的单一预测因素，而其他因素（韵律线索、根据上下文猜测）在听力中发挥更大作用。这解释了为什么路线图在早期阶段优先安排词汇学习，并强调以阅读为基础的沉浸。"}},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Recent work by <a href=\"https://global.oup.com/academic/product/how-vocabulary-is-learned-9780194403559?cc=dk&amp;lang=en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Webb and Nation (2017)</a> clarifies that there's no single point at which you \"know\" a word. Rather, vocabulary knowledge is multidimensional and develops across receptive (understanding) and productive (using) dimensions. The milestones in this article track primarily receptive vocabulary, which is why productive fluency comes later and requires additional practice through output.","zh":"<a href=\"https://global.oup.com/academic/product/how-vocabulary-is-learned-9780194403559?cc=dk&amp;lang=en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Webb 和 Nation（2017）</a>的近期研究阐明了：不存在某一个明确节点，能让你从此“认识”一个词。词汇知识是多维度的，会在接受性（理解）和产出性（使用）两个维度上不断发展。本文中的里程碑主要追踪接受性词汇，因此产出流利度会在之后出现，并需要通过输出进行额外练习。"}}],"cards":[]}}]
